# CaptureLcd  (CPU 0x4841563b)
#   The LCD SCREEN CAPTURE chord (accumulator 0x110000) -- the ONLY debug
#   primitive in the firmware that writes a file. The entry at 0x4841563B
#   picks one of two bodies (0x4841565E / 0x4841580A) on a mode word, then:
#   sprintf(name, "LCDCAP%02d.BMP" @0x4859E2A4, (*0x50021FC8)++), fopen
#   with mode "wb" @0x4859E2B4, emit a BMP header, then read the source
#   and fwrite it.
#   The SOURCE IS HARDCODED: `add 0x9ce00000, a0` at 0x4841577E, i.e. the
#   composited 640-pixel-wide LCD framebuffer, unpacked from RGB565 by the
#   5/6/5 field extraction that follows. It is NOT the MEMORY DUMP address
#   slots -- capturing the hex viewer captures the RENDERED SCREEN, 256
#   inspected bytes as ASCII text, so this is not a bulk ROM-dump path.
#   The file lands on whatever media the current-drive selector 0x5007125A
#   names, because the fopen thunk 0x48532353 indexes a per-backend vtable
#   (FDD prepends "A:\\" @0x4866472C, SD prepends "C:\\" @0x4868E2E0) and
#   CaptureLcd passes a bare filename.
#   DEAD on PROGRAM 941: its chord branch at 0x484148E4 requires
#   GetRomConfigByte() == 0xFF and ours reads 0x16 -- the exact INVERSE of
#   the gate that opens the MEMORY DUMP hex viewer. The byte is the release
#   number (0x13 = v1.3 ... 0x16 = v1.6); only the unnumbered SP7001 build
#   942 has 0xFF. Two triggers: the four corner LCD soft keys (chord
#   0x110000) and DISPLAY HOLD + PAD 1 + PAD 2 (gate at 0x484149E5).
CaptureLcd:
    add     -8, sp
